  m_Name: 1_Definition
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  ImageLeft: {fileID: 0}
  Title: Definition
  Description: Antibiotics are employed against bacterial as well as some parasitic
    infections. They have either a bacteriostatic or a bactericidal effect and can
    be effective against a small group of bacteria (narrow-spectrum) or a wide range
    of pathogens (broad-spectrum). Most antibiotics work by inhibiting cell wall synthesis,
    protein synthesis, or nucleic acid synthesis in bacteria. Common side effects
    include allergies and cross-reacting hypersensitivities, as well as nephrotoxic
    and hepatotoxic effects.
  ImageRight: {fileID: 0}
